If the fountain dries up I just engrave, stepping away isn't
necessary. If it doesn't dry up, I can usually survive a bite or two
while I step to a spare space. If there's no spare space to step to
(and I don't have /oteleport or ?oScare monster or some other handy
get-out-of-jail-free card) I die. But that's pretty rare. If I lose
the occasional character in this way on Dlvl1 before I've had a chance
to bond, I don't mind because sometimes I score an early wish and if I
waited until I had a /of Cancellation I'd *never* get any water to
bless. It's hard enough finding an altar, let alone a /oC as well...

> or if the water demon summons a friend that you can't really run away 
> from.

That's never happened to me. Actually, I might have had the occasional
succubus/ incubus summoned, but I've never seen anything scarier than
that summoned by a water demon. I tend to explore the level fully
before #dipping, so if it is a demon I can run on the next level in
the knowledge I haven't missed any good stuff.

You want to know something else? I always kick/quaff sinks with my
wizards as well, even XPlvl1 wizards. Again, the benefits outweigh the
risks, as long as you know how to manage the negative results.
- Ring. Yippee!
- The pipes break... fountain; Not a problem, I like fountains=-)
- Sewer rats: Not too dangerous, especially if my stupid kitten hasn't
run nto a rock trap yet.
- Foocubus: Yeah, can be a pain, but can also yield good rewards. If I
can't kill them easily after the first encounter, I just run straight
for the next level and come back later for a little closure on the
affair.
- Black pudding: Retreat, whack. Retreat, whack. The wizard's starting
weapon and armour are both unharmed by the pudding's rot attack, so
just retreat-whack-repeat until the pudding is dead. As long as you've
mapped (and lit) a good escape route and nothing sneaks up behind you
and blocks your path, even a XPlvl1 wizard can kill a pudding without
taking a single hit (which is good, because a single hit is deadly to
a lvl1 wizard). However the rewards are substantial: Even if it's the
first thing you kill, a black pudding will advance a XPlvl1 wizard a
full XPlevel, which will provide enough HP to withstand the "stomach
acid" when eating the corpse for lots of nutrition and a handy
intrinsic.

I usually kick sinks until I've had a ring and a pudding, unless I
have to abandon the level to a foocubus. I lose a few characters to
puddings and sometimes a level/ some gold/ some power to a foocubus,
but I get away with it much more often than not.

